WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:06.570
RAE WANG: Good afternoon.

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:09.360
Let's get started.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.704
As you think about
migrating to the cloud,

00:00:11.704 --> 00:00:13.870
one of the first words that
often comes to your mind

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:15.821
is security.

00:00:15.821 --> 00:00:17.320
Now, this week and
next you're going

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.060
to hear about
exciting technologies

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.220
on things like encryption
or DDoS prevention,

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:24.440
but at the center
of cloud security

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:28.080
there is a critical
element named people that

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.510
are the best security tools.

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:31.930
Technology is only
useful if you can

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:34.630
take control of
what a company owns,

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:38.590
how resources are provisioned,
and who has access to what.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:40.817
And that is our topic today.

00:00:40.817 --> 00:00:41.650
My name is Rae Wang.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:43.312
I'm a product manager at Google.

00:00:43.312 --> 00:00:44.770
You are also going
to meet a number

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.310
of other amazing speakers
throughout our session.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.050
So cloud brings a
lot of benefits--

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:55.720
scale, availability, agility.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:58.930
We all love building on cloud,
and that's why you are here.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:00.850
When you move to the
cloud, it's not just

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:04.540
about moving your VMs from
one data center to another.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.770
It is also the opportunity to
adopt the agile DevOps culture

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:12.044
and to transform into
a cloud based company.

00:01:12.044 --> 00:01:13.460
You want to unleash
the developers

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.300
and let them run fast
with the scale of cloud.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.430
But to do that with
a peace of mind,

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.560
you also want to put
in the guardrails

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.510
so that speed does not risk
security and compliance.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:27.610
As you move from
on-premise to cloud,

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:32.190
the one thing you definitely
don't want to lose is control.

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:34.430
So what are some of the
common resource control

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:35.679
needs on cloud?

00:01:35.679 --> 00:01:37.220
I want to share a
few stories that we

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.580
have heard from cloud admins.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:41.870
One cloud admin
came to me and said,

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:44.510
back when things
were on-premise,

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:47.810
it was very easy for him to
account for all the assets

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:49.520
that his company owned.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.200
So when he had to
build an inventory

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.320
to do scans or
audits, he was pretty

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.690
sure he included everything.

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:58.610
But on cloud it is a bit
of a different story,

00:01:58.610 --> 00:01:59.984
because you have
many developers.

00:01:59.984 --> 00:02:02.600
And one of them could just
pull out a credit card

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.310
to create a new
account, a new project,

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.020
and spin up a bunch
of VMs on cloud

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.419
without the admin knowing.

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:09.669
So what did this admin do?

00:02:09.669 --> 00:02:13.070
Well, he ran a quarterly
cloud amnesty day

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:15.590
where his developers
were asked to come clean

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:17.960
with the newly created
projects and accounts

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:22.044
and grant him access so he could
include them in his inventory.

00:02:22.044 --> 00:02:23.960
But even with that he
still wasn't really sure

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.180
he got them all.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.534
Cloud gives us a
lot of flexibility.

00:02:28.534 --> 00:02:30.200
You can choose from
many different types

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:34.910
of VMs, different VM images,
many different services.

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:38.210
But just like in real life, we
know that more choices is not

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:39.350
always better.

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:42.110
In a company, often
standardization

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:45.710
is what's required to
meet compliance needs,

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:47.960
or reduce security
risks, or even

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:50.480
to optimize test investment.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.610
So our challenge is
how to standardize

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:54.830
resource configurations
on the ever more

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:57.277
flexible public cloud.

00:02:57.277 --> 00:02:59.360
Another hot topic lately,
you probably have heard,

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:02.020
is data exfiltration.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:04.880
In the last few years, there
has been no shortage of news

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:08.300
on companies accidentally
leaking sensitive customer

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:09.950
data.

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:13.730
On one hand, cloud eliminates
one of the key risks,

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:15.980
which is leaking data
via physical device,

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:19.190
because you simply cannot
take a USB key and plug

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:20.990
into the cloud.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:23.920
On the other hand, though,
now how you access resources

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.570
is through cloud API endpoints.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:29.300
And these are APIs that
are publicly documented

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.640
and everybody can
attempt to access them.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:37.529
Therefore, your main line of
defense is now access policies.

00:03:37.529 --> 00:03:39.320
Therefore, the principle
of least privilege

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.644
is more important than ever.

00:03:41.644 --> 00:03:43.310
Next, we're going to
look at how the GCP

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:47.560
tools will help you to take
control of your resources.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.710
So first we're going
to talk about IAM.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:53.620
IAM stands for identity
and access management.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:56.730
It allows you to define
a policy that says who

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:59.450
can do what on which resources.

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:02.250
The can do what part is
defined by an IAM role which

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.530
is a group of permissions.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:06.390
To do a meaningful
job, for example,

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:09.030
if you want to let somebody
manage your GC resources,

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:11.400
it takes more than
just a single command.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.550
They need to be able
to create and delete,

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:16.440
and start and stop, and do a
bunch of things to the VMs.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.266
So we group them
together as an IAM role.

00:04:19.266 --> 00:04:20.640
Now, in the example
here, you can

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.450
see we're granting the
Compute Instance Admin

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:27.840
role to a number of
users on our project.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.530
That allows these users to
be able to go in and manage

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:34.120
all the GC instances
in that project.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:37.470
So we first launched IAM
last year at GCP Next,

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:40.860
and since then we have shipped
many more predefined roles.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.410
We're now at over
60 roles across most

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.480
of our major services.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:49.080
By using these roles instead
of the primitive owner, editor,

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.810
and viewers, you can grant
more granular permissions.

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:55.480
For example, if you want to
give your developers permissions

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.414
to look at logs so
they can do debugging,

00:04:58.414 --> 00:05:00.580
but you don't really want
them to poke around the PI

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:03.450
data on your GCS buckets, you
can grant them a log viewer

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:07.090
role on project so that the only
thing they can use in a project

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:08.770
is logs.

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:12.070
Now, I can grant these roles on
projects or organization, which

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:13.750
we will talk about
a little later.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:15.670
In some cases, you
can also grant them

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:17.510
on individual
resources-- for example,

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:19.700
on GCS buckets or
BigQuery data sets.

00:05:23.631 --> 00:05:25.880
The top question we got after
last year's launch after

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:29.790
our 20-something
pre-defined roles was--

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:31.740
can I create my
own custom roles?

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:34.250
Even at 60 roles, we still
have customers who come to us

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:36.208
and go, I just need to
tweak that a little bit.

00:05:36.208 --> 00:05:37.850
Can I have my own custom roles?

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:40.700
Today we're happy to announce
that IAM Custom Roles has

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:42.710
launched to a
public alpha, which

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:47.660
means you can go to the doc site
and sign up to be whitelisted.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:50.790
You can create custom roles
by either grouping together

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:53.540
a set of permissions or
by modifying one or more

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:55.592
of the existing roles.

00:05:55.592 --> 00:05:57.050
For example, if in
your company you

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:00.310
want to let your developers
manage GCE instances,

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:03.470
but you don't want them
to open up external IPs,

00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:05.300
you can create a
more restrictive role

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:07.190
by taking the
instance admin role

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:10.620
and removing the permission
to create external IP.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:12.980
Or maybe you have a service
account whose sole job

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:16.520
is to attach PDs to VMs.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:18.050
They can create
a very small role

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:21.260
with just that permission
for a service account.

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:23.780
Custom roles can be
created at a project level

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:25.880
to be used by
application on your team,

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:28.700
or they can also be created
at the organization level

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:30.770
and then all the developers
and all the projects

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:32.870
in your company can
access the same roles.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:37.810
Since the I in IAM
stands for identity

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:39.810
I want to mention it here,
even though we're not

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:42.130
going to go into details,
because it's covered

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:44.440
by a couple of other sessions.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:47.860
Yesterday there was a session on
Google's Identity as a Service

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:51.640
that talks about how to manage
users, groups and federations.

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:53.680
If you missed it, don't
worry, the recording

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:55.229
will be on YouTube
in a few days.

00:06:55.229 --> 00:06:57.520
And tomorrow there is going
to be a session on the best

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:00.239
practice of managing IAM
for GCE during which we're

00:07:00.239 --> 00:07:02.530
going to go into depth about
how to secure your service

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:03.029
accounts.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:07.130
So we talked about
the permissions.

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:09.790
Well, these permissions
are assigned on resources.

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:12.600
So it's important to
talk about how resources

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:16.360
are defined and grouped on GCP.

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:18.970
What you're looking at here
is a resource hierarchy.

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:23.380
Because we know that they're not
just a flat list of VMs and GCS

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:25.780
buckets, we don't want to
manage them one by one,

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:27.410
so we want to group
them together.

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:29.382
Now, if you're already
a GCP customer,

00:07:29.382 --> 00:07:31.090
you're probably familiar
with the concept

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:34.510
of a project, which is this
yellow layer in the tree.

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:37.690
A project is a basic
resource container for GCP,

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:40.520
and it's also a policy unit.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:43.900
On the top of the tree, the blue
layer-- that's the organization

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:46.480
node which we also
launched last year.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:48.430
This is your company's
root node for managing

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:50.650
all of your GCP resources.

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:53.012
Now, since then we have
added the red layers

00:07:53.012 --> 00:07:54.220
in the middle called folders.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:56.250
Because many of you
came to us and said,

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:58.090
well, my company
is large, we have

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:01.000
multiple independent divisions,
branches, departments,

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:02.962
and we want to manage
them differently.

00:08:02.962 --> 00:08:04.420
So we have added
these layers so we

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:06.490
can model your departments,
your organizations

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:08.890
within organizations.

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:11.590
IAM policies are
inherited from top

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:13.900
down in this resource hierarchy.

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:17.020
If you have the admin permission
on the organization node

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:19.840
that gives you admin permission
into all the folders, projects,

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:22.420
and resources in
your organization.

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:24.610
At any of the nodes,
the permission

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:27.520
that you get on a resource is a
combination of the permissions

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:29.440
that are directly
assigned to the resource

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:33.429
as well as all the inherited
permissions from its ancestors.

00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:35.650
The reason we do that
is so that it's not

00:08:35.650 --> 00:08:37.929
possible for a resource
owner to lock out

00:08:37.929 --> 00:08:40.720
a central admin who is granted
permissions on a high level.

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:46.480
So let's look at organization
node a little bit more.

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:48.830
Organization node, as we
said, is the root node

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:50.900
of your GCP resources.

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:54.010
So all of your other
projects, folders, resources,

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:56.560
can belong to your
organization node.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:59.610
One recent addition is
the billing account.

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:01.090
So now billing
accounts can also be

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:02.980
moved underneath your
organization node

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:06.197
and get the same
inherited IAM permissions.

00:09:06.197 --> 00:09:07.780
If you have multiple
billing accounts,

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:10.250
all you need to do is to
assign your billing admin team

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:13.051
the billing admin role once
on the organization level,

00:09:13.051 --> 00:09:15.300
and that gives them access
to all the billing accounts

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:18.280
underneath the organization.

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:22.420
The organization node is
linked to your G Suite domain.

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:25.060
That allows us to enforce
the ownership policy which

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:27.730
says, if any identity
in your company

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:29.530
goes and creates
a new project, it

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:31.750
will automatically belong
to your organization node.

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:33.250
You don't have to
do any extra work.

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:36.020
It will just being enforced.

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:38.569
It prevents shadow
projects, rogue admins,

00:09:38.569 --> 00:09:40.360
you can't have projects
created without you

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:42.910
knowing or having visibility.

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:45.610
The number of organization-wide
management tasks

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:48.070
have also become easier
with the org node.

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:51.370
For example, as an admin you
can control who in your company

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:54.190
is or is not allowed
to create resources

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:57.970
by carefully assigning
the project creator role.

00:09:57.970 --> 00:09:59.780
You can also now find
and build inventory--

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:01.370
you remember at the beginning
of the talk we talked about

00:10:01.370 --> 00:10:03.520
the admin not knowing
inventory-- now

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:06.010
you can build it by
programmatically enumerating

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:08.260
through the projects and
the folders' projects

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:11.650
and resources underneath
the organization node.

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:14.710
Another common practice is
to have a service account

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:17.800
with org level permission to
do the company-wide config

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:19.617
monitoring and enforcement.

00:10:19.617 --> 00:10:21.700
And that is in fact what
our Google's own security

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:23.610
team does.

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:25.680
Folders, as we said,
are organizations

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:26.555
within organizations.

00:10:26.555 --> 00:10:29.230
So all of these
management benefits

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:32.290
can also be done at a folder
level for your department

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:32.790
admins.

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:38.870
One new thing we want to
announce that we have recently

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:43.640
added to the organization node
is the organization policy.

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:45.380
We talked about
flexibility and the need

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:47.350
to standardize configurations.

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:51.200
Well, the organization policy
allows you to do exactly that.

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:54.949
You can define the org policy
on the organization node.

00:10:54.949 --> 00:10:57.240
It will get inherited into
other projects and resources

00:10:57.240 --> 00:10:58.310
underneath.

00:10:58.310 --> 00:11:01.290
You can also make exceptions
on a per project basis,

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:03.920
but even the exceptions are
controlled by the org-level

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:06.650
policy admin so that project
owners cannot just create

00:11:06.650 --> 00:11:09.020
a loophole for themselves.

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:11.120
Now, what's different
from the IAM policy

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:14.240
is that the IAM policy
distinguishes different users

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:15.760
with different permissions.

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:19.370
Whereas the org policies are
assigned on resources and they

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:22.360
apply to all the users who
access the resource, including

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:24.840
a project owner.

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:26.860
Let's look at one
of the policies

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:28.440
in a little more detail.

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:30.636
This is the Allowed
Services policy.

00:11:30.636 --> 00:11:32.010
What this policy
allows you to do

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:37.830
is inside a project you can go
and enable many different APIs

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:40.770
representing different services
that you can use in a project.

00:11:40.770 --> 00:11:43.170
This policy allows the
central admin to say,

00:11:43.170 --> 00:11:45.210
here are the only
allowed services

00:11:45.210 --> 00:11:48.720
that can be enabled in
a company's projects.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:51.160
In this example
here, the admin says,

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:53.610
I'm only allowing two services
to be used in our projects.

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:56.220
Now, you can do this either
for compliance reasons

00:11:56.220 --> 00:11:59.070
or you can do this for
cost control reasons.

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:01.890
This policy then gets
inherited into our projects.

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:03.750
And then when a
developer attempts

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:08.671
to enable a service that's
not allowed by the policy,

00:12:08.671 --> 00:12:10.170
they would get a
clear error message

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:13.662
indicating that this is
prevented by the org admin.

00:12:13.662 --> 00:12:15.370
Now, they have a couple
of projects where

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:16.690
you need to make exceptions.

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:20.340
So Project A is a more
sensitive, critical application

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:23.370
that needs to have a
more restrictive policy.

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:25.590
The admin sets the
exception to say,

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:27.750
I'm going to replace
the inherited policy

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:30.090
by a new policy that's
more restrictive that

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:32.550
only allows a single service.

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:35.520
Project B is a test
environment, where

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:38.250
you test out new services
before you release them

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:40.060
to your developers.

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:42.930
So in Project B, the
exception that's set

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:46.470
says, let's inherit
the parents' policy,

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:49.030
but also augment it by
adding two other services

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:51.390
so Project B can use up
to four services now.

00:12:54.950 --> 00:12:57.420
We went through, you
saw many different IAM

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:59.720
roles, different
types of policies,

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:02.840
and you also have a lot of
users, a lot of resources.

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:04.610
And we all know
managing those manually

00:13:04.610 --> 00:13:06.410
quickly becomes unscalable.

00:13:06.410 --> 00:13:08.030
We wanted to go
home for the weekend

00:13:08.030 --> 00:13:09.590
and have dinner with our family.

00:13:09.590 --> 00:13:12.260
So automation and
templatization is

00:13:12.260 --> 00:13:16.570
going to be very key to
your management experience.

00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:18.250
We all know that
the way to deploy

00:13:18.250 --> 00:13:20.380
an application, or
deploy a VM, is not

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:22.420
by manually clicking buttons.

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:25.750
You want to templatize them
to avoid unique snowflakes.

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:29.189
And now the same can be done
with project provisioning.

00:13:29.189 --> 00:13:30.730
In a company, it's
typical that there

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:33.850
are a set of centrally defined
configurations for projects,

00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:36.640
such as how they should be
named, which APIs are enabled,

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:38.590
which billing account
to use, and the default

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:41.110
IAM networking policies.

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:44.350
You want to set these
configurations once centrally.

00:13:44.350 --> 00:13:47.080
And then developers, as
they spin up new projects,

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:50.290
don't have to go and think
about it every single time.

00:13:50.290 --> 00:13:52.300
Now all the APIs
for these key steps

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:53.900
are available in the public.

00:13:53.900 --> 00:13:55.570
You can write your
program, your script,

00:13:55.570 --> 00:13:57.970
to program against these APIs.

00:13:57.970 --> 00:14:00.880
You can also wrap up your
configuration into a template

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:05.890
and have Deployment Manager
drive the automation for you.

00:14:05.890 --> 00:14:08.050
So now that you have
your resources deployed,

00:14:08.050 --> 00:14:10.258
you still want to know what's
going on in production.

00:14:10.258 --> 00:14:11.960
You don't want to fly blind.

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:13.630
And you can leverage
the autologs

00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:16.390
to keep an eye on what's going
on in the production system.

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:22.510
The admin logs now capture all
the policy changes, including

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:24.252
IAM networking and so on.

00:14:24.252 --> 00:14:26.460
So there are a couple of
things you can do with that.

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:30.430
One is you can use Stackdriver
log-based metric to,

00:14:30.430 --> 00:14:34.155
for example, set up a
metric on the event org dot

00:14:34.155 --> 00:14:35.860
set time policy.

00:14:35.860 --> 00:14:38.560
And then you can set up an alert
in Stackdriver on that metric,

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:41.962
so that every time the org
level IAM policy is changed,

00:14:41.962 --> 00:14:42.670
you get notified.

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:44.990
You get alerted for that.

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:48.250
You can also export your
audit logs into Pub/Sub

00:14:48.250 --> 00:14:51.250
and then use Pub/Sub to
trigger Cloud Functions.

00:14:51.250 --> 00:14:53.500
Cloud Functions
is a product that

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:56.810
allows you to define if this,
then that type of rules.

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:59.260
So if you want to
prevent an identity

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:02.230
outside of your company from
being added to your company's

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:05.770
IAM policies, you can define a
rule to look for that pattern

00:15:05.770 --> 00:15:09.940
and then trigger the action
to revert the policy change.

00:15:09.940 --> 00:15:12.370
We also have data access
logs which capture

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:13.630
all the data access records.

00:15:13.630 --> 00:15:16.750
So every time you use access
data it will be captured.

00:15:16.750 --> 00:15:20.350
You can export the data
access logs into BigQuery.

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:22.540
Then using BigQuery you
can answer questions

00:15:22.540 --> 00:15:27.070
such as when was the last time
person X accessed resource Y,

00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:28.880
and this allows you
to do two things.

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:31.710
One is if there are
unexpected access patterns,

00:15:31.710 --> 00:15:34.600
a user shouldn't have access to
something, you can catch that

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:36.100
and then you can
figure out where

00:15:36.100 --> 00:15:37.930
you need to fix their policy.

00:15:37.930 --> 00:15:39.460
Or alternatively,
you can find out

00:15:39.460 --> 00:15:41.668
that certain policies are
just not getting exercised.

00:15:41.668 --> 00:15:43.043
Person X could
access resource Y,

00:15:43.043 --> 00:15:44.501
but they haven't
done it for years,

00:15:44.501 --> 00:15:45.985
so it's time to
retire that policy.

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:49.280
OK.

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:51.113
So we just went through
the building blocks.

00:15:51.113 --> 00:15:54.120
You heard about the different
APIs and tools and policies.

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:56.360
Now let's put them
together end to end and do

00:15:56.360 --> 00:15:59.870
a few policy design exercises
for a few companies.

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:01.555
You can see that by
using the same tools

00:16:01.555 --> 00:16:03.890
and the same policies
configured differently,

00:16:03.890 --> 00:16:05.270
you can build
resource management

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:08.330
systems for companies with
very different priorities

00:16:08.330 --> 00:16:10.190
and cultures.

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:12.980
First, we're going to talk
about a traditional enterprise.

00:16:12.980 --> 00:16:16.850
A traditional enterprise likely
has existed and succeeded

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:20.060
in industry for quite a
while, so they have built up

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:22.520
a sophisticated
organization structure

00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:26.170
because of growth and because
of mergers, acquisitions.

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:29.060
They tend to care a lot
about security, compliance,

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:31.430
and separation of duty.

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:33.410
They also tend to
have a lot of existing

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:36.920
tools and internal systems,
and even on-premise departments

00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:41.000
that the cloud deployment
needs to be able to fit in.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:45.060
So this is an example design
for a traditional enterprise.

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:48.420
The identities can be centrally
managed in their existing LDAP

00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:50.600
services, and then
these identities

00:16:50.600 --> 00:16:54.562
can be fed right into Google
via the Identity Sync.

00:16:54.562 --> 00:16:56.020
An organization
node is provisioned

00:16:56.020 --> 00:16:58.050
on top linked with
the entity domain,

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:00.050
so that you can have full
visibility and control

00:17:00.050 --> 00:17:02.600
of the company's resources.

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:04.780
For a traditional enterprise,
procurement decisions

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:07.609
are often made at
organizational level,

00:17:07.609 --> 00:17:10.670
and the creation and
changing of resources

00:17:10.670 --> 00:17:13.940
are done centrally
by existing tools,

00:17:13.940 --> 00:17:16.130
perhaps through a
service account.

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:18.109
Therefore, developers
don't really

00:17:18.109 --> 00:17:21.401
need permissions to go and
create or change resources.

00:17:21.401 --> 00:17:23.609
They can request those to
be done through an existing

00:17:23.609 --> 00:17:25.089
workflow.

00:17:25.089 --> 00:17:27.920
Folders are created, as we
talked about, your model, maybe

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:30.410
your different
international branches

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:32.480
or different independent groups.

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:36.180
They can be used to grant
these different divisions,

00:17:36.180 --> 00:17:41.144
different policies, as well as
a way to do cost aggregation.

00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:47.530
One other practice that
we have commonly seen

00:17:47.530 --> 00:17:51.770
is to pre-create groups
for all the major IAM roles

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:54.670
that you want to use at your
project and folder level.

00:17:54.670 --> 00:17:56.680
And then post the
initial provisioning,

00:17:56.680 --> 00:17:59.290
you can even drive all of
your IAM policy changes

00:17:59.290 --> 00:18:02.216
throughout our system.

00:18:02.216 --> 00:18:03.840
So that was the
traditional enterprise.

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:08.070
Now let's go to the other side,
which is a very Agile Dev/Ops

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:09.700
company.

00:18:09.700 --> 00:18:12.420
So in the Agile Dev/Ops
team, the priority

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:16.620
is often to optimize for
team autonomy and velocity.

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:18.982
You want to let your
developers run fast,

00:18:18.982 --> 00:18:21.440
and your developers are trusted
to make their own decisions

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:25.620
on what tools to use and how
to deploy and monitor the apps.

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:27.020
But like we said,
the admin still

00:18:27.020 --> 00:18:30.120
wants to set in some guardrails
so that the developers are

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:32.100
still operating within
the bounds of security

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:34.980
and compliance.

00:18:34.980 --> 00:18:37.110
Here, underneath the
organization node,

00:18:37.110 --> 00:18:39.840
you can provision a folder for
different teams' departments.

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:43.170
What's different here is that
these teams and departments

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:46.740
fully own all the resources and
how to deploy and manage them

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:48.870
underneath their folders.

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:51.450
Of course, there are some
centrally shared infrastructure

00:18:51.450 --> 00:18:53.840
resources that don't belong
to these individual teams,

00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:56.910
such as your cross-product
project networks or Stackdriver

00:18:56.910 --> 00:18:58.020
accounts.

00:18:58.020 --> 00:19:02.010
So those can be curated in
shared infrastructure projects

00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:05.795
or folders and only managed
by your central networking

00:19:05.795 --> 00:19:06.545
or security teams.

00:19:08.939 --> 00:19:11.230
Here it seems the developers
get to do their own things

00:19:11.230 --> 00:19:13.130
and make their own choices.

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:16.450
The admin has set the
organization-wide org policies

00:19:16.450 --> 00:19:20.491
to set those guardrails, so that
only the blessed config options

00:19:20.491 --> 00:19:22.490
are available to the
developers when they choose

00:19:22.490 --> 00:19:25.480
to deploy their applications.

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:28.630
The service account that
has org-wide permission

00:19:28.630 --> 00:19:30.271
takes a slightly
less active role

00:19:30.271 --> 00:19:32.770
and no longer is in the critical
path to deploy applications

00:19:32.770 --> 00:19:36.340
for developers, but instead
it does org-wide aggregation

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:39.840
and monitoring.

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:41.950
A third type of company
that we commonly see

00:19:41.950 --> 00:19:45.310
is an education or
a training company.

00:19:45.310 --> 00:19:48.550
In such an organization,
the training owner usually

00:19:48.550 --> 00:19:51.160
owns the billing
account and all the cost

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:54.040
that's associated
with running the labs.

00:19:54.040 --> 00:19:55.870
Isolation between
both the users,

00:19:55.870 --> 00:19:59.860
and between the users and
lab owners, is very critical.

00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:01.450
The entire provisioning
process needs

00:20:01.450 --> 00:20:07.020
to be fully automated end to end
so it can be fast and scalable.

00:20:07.020 --> 00:20:09.900
In this case, the lab owner
can use a G Suite domain

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:13.110
to host the identities
of the lab users

00:20:13.110 --> 00:20:15.960
and use the org node and folders
to host the resources used

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:17.350
for the labs.

00:20:17.350 --> 00:20:19.770
For every new session,
a temporary user ID

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:24.810
can be created and approached
[AUDIO OUT] correspondingly.

00:20:24.810 --> 00:20:27.900
The user's activities are fully
contained within the project

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:29.382
and that creates isolation.

00:20:29.382 --> 00:20:30.840
And at the end of
the session, it's

00:20:30.840 --> 00:20:35.040
very easy to just wipe out
both the project and the user.

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:38.190
One thing that a lot of lab
owners are very concerned about

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:38.970
is abuse.

00:20:38.970 --> 00:20:41.397
Because when you're
paying for those VMs,

00:20:41.397 --> 00:20:43.230
you certainly do not
want people to be using

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:45.540
them to do Bitcoin mining.

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:47.755
So there are a few
ways to help you

00:20:47.755 --> 00:20:49.660
to address the abuse concern.

00:20:49.660 --> 00:20:51.970
One is that projects
come with default quotas.

00:20:51.970 --> 00:20:53.640
So unless you raise
them, the defaults

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:56.670
are set low enough to prevent
a user from spinning up

00:20:56.670 --> 00:21:00.490
a large amount of resources
to abuse your resources.

00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:02.340
You can also set
organization policy,

00:21:02.340 --> 00:21:04.320
as we talk about the
allowed services policy,

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:06.720
to restrict every lab to
only be able to enable

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:09.810
the necessary
services for the labs.

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:12.420
And then the identity
domain allows the admin

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:15.480
to have the last say here,
because only the admin,

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:17.920
not the users, can
control the identities.

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:21.480
So if the admin comes in
and disables or deletes

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:24.450
an identity, that immediately
removes all the access

00:21:24.450 --> 00:21:27.105
that a user can have
to these resources.

00:21:27.105 --> 00:21:28.980
And since the user can't
come back and change

00:21:28.980 --> 00:21:30.730
it back there won't
be any race condition,

00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:34.230
the admin has the last say.

00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:38.140
How many have you have tried
the security boot camps?

00:21:38.140 --> 00:21:39.250
Raise of hand.

00:21:39.250 --> 00:21:40.080
A few.

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:42.420
So the security boot
camps were actually

00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:44.260
built on this architecture.

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:46.260
They were built by this
company called Qwiklabs,

00:21:46.260 --> 00:21:48.900
who is a training company
and also a recent Google

00:21:48.900 --> 00:21:50.195
acquisition.

00:21:50.195 --> 00:21:51.570
So if you need to
provide courses

00:21:51.570 --> 00:21:53.220
in labs in the
future on GCP, you

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:55.380
can either use Qwiklabs
as the platform

00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:58.800
or use this reference
architecture.

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:02.130
So we have just said that using
the same tools and policies

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:03.900
configured differently,
you can cater

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:05.460
to three very
different companies

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:07.680
with different
priorities and culture.

00:22:07.680 --> 00:22:09.600
Next, let's invite
on stage my colleague

00:22:09.600 --> 00:22:11.060
Ray Colline to
show you the demos.

00:22:11.060 --> 00:22:13.012
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:20.332 --> 00:22:23.405
RAY COLLINE: All
right, thank you Rae.

00:22:23.405 --> 00:22:25.280
I'm actually Ray Colline,
a software engineer

00:22:25.280 --> 00:22:27.410
who's worked a lot on the
organizations and folders

00:22:27.410 --> 00:22:28.640
products.

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:30.440
In this demo, we
are going to show

00:22:30.440 --> 00:22:33.670
how a hypothetical
G-twix corporation can

00:22:33.670 --> 00:22:38.910
use GCP to lower their costs
and gain a competitive advantage

00:22:38.910 --> 00:22:40.710
in the candy bar industry.

00:22:40.710 --> 00:22:45.240
Let's explore how they
can use the flexibility

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:48.390
of this hierarchy to organize
and gain control over their GCP

00:22:48.390 --> 00:22:50.100
resources.

00:22:50.100 --> 00:22:53.230
To produce the two different
bars in every package,

00:22:53.230 --> 00:22:56.880
the G-twix corporation has
organized themselves into two

00:22:56.880 --> 00:22:58.020
divisions--

00:22:58.020 --> 00:23:01.080
left G-twix and right G-twix.

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:03.660
These two divisions have very
different corporate cultures

00:23:03.660 --> 00:23:05.580
and requirements.

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:07.770
We will show you how you
can use the GCP resource

00:23:07.770 --> 00:23:10.230
hierarchy, IAM policies.

00:23:10.230 --> 00:23:15.420
G Suite groups, and
service accounts

00:23:15.420 --> 00:23:21.430
to support a configuration that
uses policy scanners, audit

00:23:21.430 --> 00:23:27.040
logging, organization policies,
and project templating to gain

00:23:27.040 --> 00:23:32.680
control over their resources.

00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:34.810
Now, here is the
Manage Resources page

00:23:34.810 --> 00:23:37.270
within the Cloud
Console, where we

00:23:37.270 --> 00:23:41.500
can get an overview of
the G-twix GCP hierarchy.

00:23:41.500 --> 00:23:47.880
Now, note, I will only be
showing the resource hierarchy

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:49.890
and their associated
IAM policies that enable

00:23:49.890 --> 00:23:53.040
all of these configurations.

00:23:53.040 --> 00:23:54.692
Now, looking at the
top level folder,

00:23:54.692 --> 00:23:56.150
we see that there's
an organization

00:23:56.150 --> 00:23:59.550
node and two folders.

00:23:59.550 --> 00:24:02.900
The first folder is for their
IT team and the second folder

00:24:02.900 --> 00:24:05.960
is for all of the
different product teams.

00:24:05.960 --> 00:24:09.720
Now, within the IT folder
we have four projects.

00:24:09.720 --> 00:24:12.800
One is the service account
container for centralization

00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:15.150
of service account management.

00:24:15.150 --> 00:24:18.830
The second is an
IT security scanner

00:24:18.830 --> 00:24:21.440
where you can run the
open source IAM policy

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:24.320
scanner and some
homegrown tools to ensure

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:28.490
that the configuration of
all the resources is correct.

00:24:28.490 --> 00:24:33.170
The third is an IT
project templating project

00:24:33.170 --> 00:24:36.740
where all of the templates that
ensure standard configurations

00:24:36.740 --> 00:24:40.010
are contained and where
they get provisioned.

00:24:40.010 --> 00:24:42.740
And fourth is an XPN
host network where

00:24:42.740 --> 00:24:44.360
all of G-twix's networks live.

00:24:47.240 --> 00:24:52.180
Now let's look at Left G-twix.

00:24:52.180 --> 00:24:56.430
There, central control is a core
requirement to their business.

00:24:56.430 --> 00:25:00.490
They value standardization to
lower costs, increase security

00:25:00.490 --> 00:25:03.820
and reliability, and to
minimize the variations that

00:25:03.820 --> 00:25:06.070
need to be evaluated against
their compliance regimes.

00:25:08.590 --> 00:25:11.020
In fact, Left G-twix
finds themselves

00:25:11.020 --> 00:25:15.242
under PCI compliance.

00:25:15.242 --> 00:25:17.200
We will now show you
their IAM policies folders

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:19.180
and project set up.

00:25:19.180 --> 00:25:23.080
So first, we can see on
their divisional folder

00:25:23.080 --> 00:25:27.790
that in the viewer role there is
the IT security scanner service

00:25:27.790 --> 00:25:28.870
account.

00:25:28.870 --> 00:25:31.009
This is inherited
from the org node

00:25:31.009 --> 00:25:32.800
and this gives the
ability for the security

00:25:32.800 --> 00:25:35.780
scanner to see every
resource within the Left

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:38.830
G-twix division.

00:25:38.830 --> 00:25:42.970
Secondly, we'll see that both
for folder creator and project

00:25:42.970 --> 00:25:47.880
creator the G-twix provisioning
system is the only one allowed

00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:50.160
to create folders and projects.

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:52.770
This guarantees that they
use the standard setups that

00:25:52.770 --> 00:25:58.510
are prescribed by
their requirements.

00:25:58.510 --> 00:26:00.460
When we go into the
folder we'll see that they

00:26:00.460 --> 00:26:01.930
have three different projects.

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:04.660
They have a dev, test,
and prod project.

00:26:04.660 --> 00:26:09.100
Now, the dev and test projects
are set up very similarly.

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:12.310
For the dev projects, we
will see that the owner

00:26:12.310 --> 00:26:14.860
is the provisioner.

00:26:14.860 --> 00:26:16.990
What this means is they
used a templating system

00:26:16.990 --> 00:26:18.790
to set up this project
to keep it standard.

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:23.680
They've given the
G Suite group that

00:26:23.680 --> 00:26:29.770
represents their dev team the
ability to have editor rights.

00:26:29.770 --> 00:26:32.020
Now, editor is a
fairly broad role,

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:34.300
and this is generally OK
because their dev and test

00:26:34.300 --> 00:26:38.540
projects do not have
customer or revenue data.

00:26:38.540 --> 00:26:42.510
Now, when we look at
the production project,

00:26:42.510 --> 00:26:46.110
we will see that the owner is
still the provisioning robot.

00:26:46.110 --> 00:26:48.120
In fact, the dev and
test and prod projects

00:26:48.120 --> 00:26:50.411
have the identical setup,
because they were provisioned

00:26:50.411 --> 00:26:52.440
with the same template.

00:26:52.440 --> 00:26:56.660
But the editor access is
only given to the current SRE

00:26:56.660 --> 00:26:58.380
on-call.

00:26:58.380 --> 00:27:02.460
We can imagine that on-premise
G-twix has an Active Directory

00:27:02.460 --> 00:27:05.490
system that's hooked up
with some group workflows,

00:27:05.490 --> 00:27:10.020
and they use calendar based
and time of day based rules

00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:11.910
for putting people into groups.

00:27:11.910 --> 00:27:14.730
And then those groups are then
synced using the identity sync

00:27:14.730 --> 00:27:19.140
features of G Suite to
push them into G Suite

00:27:19.140 --> 00:27:20.432
so we can use them here.

00:27:20.432 --> 00:27:22.890
And so this means that only
the current on-call is actually

00:27:22.890 --> 00:27:25.300
given access to this group.

00:27:25.300 --> 00:27:28.800
Secondly, we'll see a
few limited curated roles

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:33.030
that we give to people
with prod access, which

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:35.640
is another similar one
of these managed groups,

00:27:35.640 --> 00:27:37.620
and we employ the principle
of least privilege

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:41.560
which is required
by PCI compliance.

00:27:41.560 --> 00:27:48.170
So let's review left G-twix.

00:27:48.170 --> 00:27:51.780
One, we prevent
users from creating

00:27:51.780 --> 00:27:56.100
projects and other resources
to avoid snowflake setups

00:27:56.100 --> 00:27:59.040
and loss of control.

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:01.360
Two, we employ the
principle of least privilege

00:28:01.360 --> 00:28:06.200
required by PCI and
other compliance regimes.

00:28:06.200 --> 00:28:09.970
Three, we use group
memberships on IAM policies

00:28:09.970 --> 00:28:12.310
and use workflow and
group integrations

00:28:12.310 --> 00:28:14.980
to control memberships.

00:28:14.980 --> 00:28:19.170
And four, we use different
policies for production and dev

00:28:19.170 --> 00:28:19.670
projects.

00:28:22.230 --> 00:28:25.560
So let's talk
about Right G-twix.

00:28:25.560 --> 00:28:27.930
They have a completely
different set of requirements

00:28:27.930 --> 00:28:30.000
for their use of GCP.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:34.300
They wish to run fast and loose
to give room for innovation.

00:28:34.300 --> 00:28:36.870
They value independence
and the ability for teams

00:28:36.870 --> 00:28:40.110
to choose the best solutions
to fit their needs.

00:28:40.110 --> 00:28:41.850
Fortunately for Right
G-twix, they're not

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:44.340
under any compliance regimes.

00:28:44.340 --> 00:28:46.950
However, the G-twix
IT department

00:28:46.950 --> 00:28:51.720
wants to ensure that they
have sufficient guardrails

00:28:51.720 --> 00:28:54.510
around their usage
to keep things safe.

00:28:54.510 --> 00:29:00.270
I will now show you the
Right G-twix organization.

00:29:00.270 --> 00:29:01.920
In their divisional
folder, we will

00:29:01.920 --> 00:29:05.970
see that again the IT
security scanner, inherited

00:29:05.970 --> 00:29:07.920
through the org node,
has viewer access

00:29:07.920 --> 00:29:10.170
and therefore can look at
all of the configurations

00:29:10.170 --> 00:29:13.860
and resources across
their division.

00:29:13.860 --> 00:29:19.620
Two, that in the folder creator
and project creator roles,

00:29:19.620 --> 00:29:23.850
their entire team group is able
to create projects and folders.

00:29:26.970 --> 00:29:31.240
Three, we let them have folder
viewer access so they can

00:29:31.240 --> 00:29:35.100
see their colleagues' folders.

00:29:35.100 --> 00:29:38.230
Now, going in here, we
will see that there's

00:29:38.230 --> 00:29:40.949
an experimental project.

00:29:40.949 --> 00:29:42.490
This experimental
project was created

00:29:42.490 --> 00:29:44.530
by one of their
engineers, Jenna Wu, who's

00:29:44.530 --> 00:29:46.840
working on their next
generation system.

00:29:46.840 --> 00:29:50.320
And we'll note that she
has owner on this project,

00:29:50.320 --> 00:29:55.830
and this lets her invite her
colleagues as she sees fit.

00:29:55.830 --> 00:30:00.160
We'll also notice when we
look at their dev project

00:30:00.160 --> 00:30:03.310
that the owner is not
the provisioner robot

00:30:03.310 --> 00:30:06.790
but instead is their dev
team, because at Right G-twix

00:30:06.790 --> 00:30:11.860
they allow for homegrown scripts
and for one-off setups, again,

00:30:11.860 --> 00:30:14.480
to fit their needs.

00:30:14.480 --> 00:30:18.180
So let's review right G-twix.

00:30:18.180 --> 00:30:21.250
One, they allow
anyone in their org

00:30:21.250 --> 00:30:25.150
to create folders and projects.

00:30:25.150 --> 00:30:28.030
Two, even with the
looser configuration,

00:30:28.030 --> 00:30:30.070
they still lock down
their production product

00:30:30.070 --> 00:30:34.150
to SRE and those
who need to know.

00:30:34.150 --> 00:30:37.960
Three, they encourage
innovation by allowing

00:30:37.960 --> 00:30:40.730
experimental projects.

00:30:40.730 --> 00:30:46.510
And four, the G-twix IT team
uses the IT security scanner

00:30:46.510 --> 00:30:49.120
to ensure that Right
G-twix does not

00:30:49.120 --> 00:30:52.840
have any large vulnerabilities
or other types of security

00:30:52.840 --> 00:30:55.780
issues.

00:30:55.780 --> 00:30:58.360
Overall, we have
seen how G-twix IT

00:30:58.360 --> 00:31:03.880
has been able to use the
organization, folders, IAM,

00:31:03.880 --> 00:31:07.840
and groups and service accounts
to configure their resources

00:31:07.840 --> 00:31:10.780
to fit their unique needs.

00:31:10.780 --> 00:31:13.090
If you're curious about
project templating,

00:31:13.090 --> 00:31:16.600
service accounts, organization
policies, group management

00:31:16.600 --> 00:31:20.320
and policy scanning, please
see us at the PM sandbox

00:31:20.320 --> 00:31:22.120
afterwards.

00:31:22.120 --> 00:31:26.450
It's great that we reviewed
this hypothetical organization,

00:31:26.450 --> 00:31:28.750
but now we bring on stage
Lukas Karlsson from the Broad

00:31:28.750 --> 00:31:32.410
Institute to give a
real world example

00:31:32.410 --> 00:31:36.190
of how they organize their GCP
resource hierarchy to achieve

00:31:36.190 --> 00:31:38.200
their organization's needs.

00:31:38.200 --> 00:31:40.220
Thank you, everyone.

00:31:40.220 --> 00:31:42.100
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:44.450 --> 00:31:46.120
LUKAS KARLSSON:
I'm Lukas Karlsson

00:31:46.120 --> 00:31:47.200
from the Broad Institute.

00:31:47.200 --> 00:31:50.200
I'm the cloud
architect and developer

00:31:50.200 --> 00:31:52.970
advocate at the Broad.

00:31:52.970 --> 00:31:57.030
The Broad is a
nonprofit genomics

00:31:57.030 --> 00:31:59.880
and biomedical
research center located

00:31:59.880 --> 00:32:02.160
in Cambridge, Massachusetts.

00:32:02.160 --> 00:32:05.790
I started there in 2003, just
after they completed the Human

00:32:05.790 --> 00:32:08.076
Genome Project.

00:32:08.076 --> 00:32:10.200
At the time, we were called
the Whitehead Institute

00:32:10.200 --> 00:32:12.210
Center for Genome Research.

00:32:12.210 --> 00:32:15.960
The Broad itself
was founded in 2004.

00:32:15.960 --> 00:32:18.152
And you may have
heard of us, if you

00:32:18.152 --> 00:32:19.860
hadn't heard of the
Human Genome Project,

00:32:19.860 --> 00:32:22.470
you may have heard of us
more recently in regards

00:32:22.470 --> 00:32:26.280
to our patent for the
CRISPR/Cas9 system for editing

00:32:26.280 --> 00:32:28.730
genes.

00:32:28.730 --> 00:32:33.880
The Broad gets it's funding
from a variety of sources.

00:32:33.880 --> 00:32:36.450
For example,
philanthropic donations,

00:32:36.450 --> 00:32:38.820
federal government grants
from the National Institutes

00:32:38.820 --> 00:32:41.540
of Health and the National
Cancer Institutes,

00:32:41.540 --> 00:32:45.180
collaborations and partnerships
with industry, and technology

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:47.870
licensing opportunities.

00:32:47.870 --> 00:32:51.180
Each of these funding sources
for us translates internally

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:53.630
into something
called a cost object.

00:32:53.630 --> 00:32:57.050
We use these cost objects to
pay for the services, materials,

00:32:57.050 --> 00:32:59.920
and staff that
support the research.

00:32:59.920 --> 00:33:02.240
Cost objects, for us,
have very specific rules

00:33:02.240 --> 00:33:04.160
about how they can be used.

00:33:04.160 --> 00:33:08.510
Before you can use your cost
object to pay for GCP services,

00:33:08.510 --> 00:33:11.000
you need to go through a
complicated approval process.

00:33:11.000 --> 00:33:13.100
You have to work with
your grant's administrator

00:33:13.100 --> 00:33:16.640
to determine if the grant can
support the volume of spend

00:33:16.640 --> 00:33:18.800
that you're looking to
do and to understand

00:33:18.800 --> 00:33:21.590
how long the grant is
available and whether it's even

00:33:21.590 --> 00:33:25.690
allowed to be used for
cloud computing services.

00:33:25.690 --> 00:33:28.210
Once you've received
approval for using your cost

00:33:28.210 --> 00:33:31.060
object in the cloud, we will
create a billing account

00:33:31.060 --> 00:33:32.440
for you.

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:35.270
Broad is set up similar
to a GCP reseller

00:33:35.270 --> 00:33:37.570
in that we have one
master billing account,

00:33:37.570 --> 00:33:40.780
and we receive a monthly
invoice from Google for that.

00:33:40.780 --> 00:33:42.880
We have dozens and
dozens of things

00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:45.970
called sub accounts which sit
underneath the master billing

00:33:45.970 --> 00:33:46.966
account.

00:33:46.966 --> 00:33:48.340
Each of these
sub-accounts for us

00:33:48.340 --> 00:33:51.910
corresponds to one of our
internal cost objects,

00:33:51.910 --> 00:33:54.700
and we have about
75 of them today.

00:33:54.700 --> 00:33:58.990
We're also a G Suite customer
and have been since 2010.

00:33:58.990 --> 00:34:01.030
Over the years, we've
actually accumulated more

00:34:01.030 --> 00:34:03.340
than one G Suite
domain, so we actually

00:34:03.340 --> 00:34:08.730
have multiple organization
resource nodes as well.

00:34:08.730 --> 00:34:10.800
We use the IAM
folders functionality

00:34:10.800 --> 00:34:13.199
to organize our folders
and to grant permissions

00:34:13.199 --> 00:34:14.610
to the projects.

00:34:14.610 --> 00:34:17.610
We map the folder structure
very similar to how

00:34:17.610 --> 00:34:19.320
we map the actual organization.

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:22.949
So in the example you see
here, the IT department

00:34:22.949 --> 00:34:26.940
has two organizations beneath
it, engagement and operations,

00:34:26.940 --> 00:34:28.710
each of which have a folder.

00:34:28.710 --> 00:34:31.440
They collect all of their
projects in that folder.

00:34:31.440 --> 00:34:36.000
In general, the person who is
the leader of that organization

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:38.850
is the folder administrator
on that folder.

00:34:38.850 --> 00:34:40.530
They can control
whether projects

00:34:40.530 --> 00:34:43.679
get put into the folder or
whether other folders can be

00:34:43.679 --> 00:34:47.460
created underneath that folder.

00:34:47.460 --> 00:34:50.290
We apply a number of
organization policies

00:34:50.290 --> 00:34:52.530
at our org node
at the top level.

00:34:52.530 --> 00:34:55.800
For example, we're rather
open about project creation.

00:34:55.800 --> 00:34:59.550
So everyone in the Institute
is able to create projects,

00:34:59.550 --> 00:35:02.430
but we're very strict
about how money gets spent.

00:35:02.430 --> 00:35:06.250
So no one is allowed to
enable billing on projects,

00:35:06.250 --> 00:35:08.250
except for specific
people who are

00:35:08.250 --> 00:35:10.590
billing account administrators.

00:35:10.590 --> 00:35:14.310
The billing accounts that
we have, all 75 of them,

00:35:14.310 --> 00:35:16.840
are in our org node as well.

00:35:16.840 --> 00:35:21.120
So we're able to apply the
billing account creator

00:35:21.120 --> 00:35:22.860
and billing account
administrator

00:35:22.860 --> 00:35:24.840
and a few other positions
at the org level,

00:35:24.840 --> 00:35:27.270
so that someone in our
procurement department

00:35:27.270 --> 00:35:29.550
can oversee all of
the billing accounts

00:35:29.550 --> 00:35:34.410
that exist and can end them
when a grant has ended.

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:36.120
We also have some
folks in IT who

00:35:36.120 --> 00:35:39.060
oversee administration
of all of the projects,

00:35:39.060 --> 00:35:41.280
and so they have
owner level access.

00:35:41.280 --> 00:35:43.170
And then, as has been
described a few times,

00:35:43.170 --> 00:35:45.480
we have a service account
that can read everything.

00:35:45.480 --> 00:35:47.550
That allows us to do
automation and security

00:35:47.550 --> 00:35:49.710
scans, and so forth.

00:35:49.710 --> 00:35:51.690
We use project labels
to some extent.

00:35:51.690 --> 00:35:54.900
Initially, we were using them
to track which cost object.

00:35:54.900 --> 00:35:57.960
But when we moved to the
billing account model,

00:35:57.960 --> 00:35:59.130
we've stopped doing that.

00:35:59.130 --> 00:36:01.560
We use them to track which
environment a project

00:36:01.560 --> 00:36:02.850
is associated with.

00:36:02.850 --> 00:36:05.050
And now that resource
labels are available,

00:36:05.050 --> 00:36:07.470
we've started using those
on some of our resources

00:36:07.470 --> 00:36:11.990
to track how much a particular
workflow might cost.

00:36:11.990 --> 00:36:13.970
So provisioning
projects in this model

00:36:13.970 --> 00:36:16.790
can be quite time consuming.

00:36:16.790 --> 00:36:20.450
I have one example that Rae and
I have discussed repeatedly.

00:36:20.450 --> 00:36:22.520
A couple of years ago,
a year or two ago,

00:36:22.520 --> 00:36:25.490
I had to create 50
projects for a workshop

00:36:25.490 --> 00:36:28.240
where each person would
have their own project.

00:36:28.240 --> 00:36:31.910
And so to do so, I
opened 50 browser tabs,

00:36:31.910 --> 00:36:35.446
and I clicked create
project 50 times.

00:36:35.446 --> 00:36:37.820
Then I proceeded to follow
all the steps that I have here

00:36:37.820 --> 00:36:38.444
on this screen.

00:36:38.444 --> 00:36:40.610
So for each one I
had to enable billing

00:36:40.610 --> 00:36:43.070
to the appropriate
billing account.

00:36:43.070 --> 00:36:46.760
For each one I had to enable
a handful of API services

00:36:46.760 --> 00:36:48.560
in the API console,
some of which

00:36:48.560 --> 00:36:50.930
you click and wait
for the service

00:36:50.930 --> 00:36:54.990
to enable before you can
click and enable the next one.

00:36:54.990 --> 00:36:58.940
We had to set about half a dozen
IAM policies on each project

00:36:58.940 --> 00:37:01.640
so that the right users
could administer the project

00:37:01.640 --> 00:37:03.260
and the right service
accounts could

00:37:03.260 --> 00:37:05.180
interact with the project.

00:37:05.180 --> 00:37:06.920
And again, we had
project labels.

00:37:09.422 --> 00:37:11.130
These projects were
all compute projects,

00:37:11.130 --> 00:37:16.610
so we had to enable the compute
usage export for each of them.

00:37:16.610 --> 00:37:19.100
Now, a new thing we do
today is set the default

00:37:19.100 --> 00:37:21.890
compute region and zone.

00:37:21.890 --> 00:37:24.110
This was a very painful
process, and this is actually

00:37:24.110 --> 00:37:24.650
something--

00:37:24.650 --> 00:37:27.650
I was doing this manually
in advance to the workshop.

00:37:27.650 --> 00:37:29.300
But the application
that we built

00:37:29.300 --> 00:37:32.250
on Google Cloud,
called firecloud.org,

00:37:32.250 --> 00:37:35.360
it has to do this as
part of the service.

00:37:35.360 --> 00:37:39.110
So there's no way that the
application can expect a person

00:37:39.110 --> 00:37:41.070
to go perform all these tasks.

00:37:41.070 --> 00:37:42.470
So we've automated this--

00:37:42.470 --> 00:37:47.309
initially we automated it in
Python using a simple script.

00:37:47.309 --> 00:37:48.725
Now this is the
same functionality

00:37:48.725 --> 00:37:51.380
that is built into
our Java application

00:37:51.380 --> 00:37:53.360
as part of our cloud service.

00:37:53.360 --> 00:37:58.370
So real quick, I'm going to
talk about the Python tool

00:37:58.370 --> 00:38:00.230
that I've made to do this.

00:38:00.230 --> 00:38:01.610
It's open source.

00:38:01.610 --> 00:38:03.330
It's available on GitHub.

00:38:03.330 --> 00:38:07.010
My username is L-U-K-W-A-M, and
I think it might be the only

00:38:07.010 --> 00:38:09.352
thing there currently.

00:38:09.352 --> 00:38:11.060
It currently uses the
application default

00:38:11.060 --> 00:38:13.940
credentials, so it's very
easy to just run it right

00:38:13.940 --> 00:38:15.155
after you do the git clone.

00:38:15.155 --> 00:38:17.030
You don't really need
to do anything special,

00:38:17.030 --> 00:38:20.780
especially if you are
on Google Cloud Shell

00:38:20.780 --> 00:38:24.140
and you already have all
of the resources installed.

00:38:24.140 --> 00:38:29.090
I currently am using a YAML
file to configure templates.

00:38:29.090 --> 00:38:31.340
For any type of project
I want to create,

00:38:31.340 --> 00:38:33.110
I can create a
YAML file that has

00:38:33.110 --> 00:38:36.690
all of the desirable
settings for that project.

00:38:36.690 --> 00:38:39.040
Here's an example
of how it would run.

00:38:39.040 --> 00:38:41.420
You basically run the
command and then specify

00:38:41.420 --> 00:38:43.880
which template you
would like to use,

00:38:43.880 --> 00:38:47.350
and you have a folder where
you store all your templates.

00:38:47.350 --> 00:38:49.430
In an upcoming
version it will also

00:38:49.430 --> 00:38:52.880
look for templates
in a GCS bucket.

00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:55.400
If you wish, you can also
avoid using the template

00:38:55.400 --> 00:38:58.400
or you can override settings in
your template with the command

00:38:58.400 --> 00:38:59.960
line options.

00:38:59.960 --> 00:39:01.820
If your template puts
all your projects

00:39:01.820 --> 00:39:04.100
in a particular folder,
but for this one

00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:06.410
you want this project to
be in a different folder,

00:39:06.410 --> 00:39:08.420
you can specify that
at the command line.

00:39:08.420 --> 00:39:09.920
And real quick, I'm
just going to go

00:39:09.920 --> 00:39:11.475
through what the
various options are

00:39:11.475 --> 00:39:13.460
that are currently supported.

00:39:13.460 --> 00:39:16.670
Upon project creation, you
can either automatically

00:39:16.670 --> 00:39:20.300
set the parent to your org node
or to one of your folder nodes

00:39:20.300 --> 00:39:23.960
using the Cloud
Resource Manager API.

00:39:23.960 --> 00:39:26.870
It enables a billing account to
the specified billing account.

00:39:26.870 --> 00:39:29.840
As I said we have 75
of them, so depending

00:39:29.840 --> 00:39:31.850
on what you're creating
you have to know which

00:39:31.850 --> 00:39:33.770
billing account to put it in.

00:39:33.770 --> 00:39:36.920
It sets whatever
desirable IAM policies

00:39:36.920 --> 00:39:40.550
on the project using the
Resource Manager API.

00:39:40.550 --> 00:39:44.840
It enables each API service,
like compute and storage,

00:39:44.840 --> 00:39:48.350
or whatever is needed for
your particular situation,

00:39:48.350 --> 00:39:50.930
using the Service
Management API.

00:39:50.930 --> 00:39:53.300
It can put project labels
on the project which

00:39:53.300 --> 00:39:56.840
we'll use, for example, if
we have three projects where

00:39:56.840 --> 00:39:59.990
one is dev, and one is
test, and one is production.

00:39:59.990 --> 00:40:01.880
You can put an
environment label on there

00:40:01.880 --> 00:40:03.960
to tell which is which.

00:40:03.960 --> 00:40:07.830
It will create service
accounts using the IAM API,

00:40:07.830 --> 00:40:11.320
and it can also create keys
and download those keys.

00:40:11.320 --> 00:40:14.370
And it can set some
default compute settings.

00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:17.580
If you specify a compute usage
bucket that doesn't exist,

00:40:17.580 --> 00:40:20.820
it will create the bucket in
GCS first and then set that

00:40:20.820 --> 00:40:22.316
as your compute usage bucket.

00:40:22.316 --> 00:40:23.940
If it does exist,
then it won't bother.

00:40:26.540 --> 00:40:28.077
Here is an example
of it running.

00:40:28.077 --> 00:40:29.660
And then real quick
I'm actually going

00:40:29.660 --> 00:40:32.490
to attempt to do this
live for everybody.

00:40:32.490 --> 00:40:35.960
So if we could switch
to the computer.

00:40:35.960 --> 00:40:39.680
Here is the Git Repo
that I mentioned.

00:40:39.680 --> 00:40:42.590
Here is a very simple
template, which

00:40:42.590 --> 00:40:46.100
literally just sets the
appropriate billing account.

00:40:46.100 --> 00:40:50.810
It sets my super admin
account as the owner,

00:40:50.810 --> 00:40:56.990
and it sets the Google group,
DevOps at Broad, as an editor.

00:40:56.990 --> 00:41:01.040
It puts a label on it, which is
the former way we used to track

00:41:01.040 --> 00:41:03.440
cost objects across projects.

00:41:03.440 --> 00:41:06.530
And it sets a folder,
which is the DevOps

00:41:06.530 --> 00:41:09.300
folder within my organization.

00:41:09.300 --> 00:41:12.100
So real quick I will just
run an example of this.

00:41:23.680 --> 00:41:25.480
The first thing it
does is print out

00:41:25.480 --> 00:41:27.430
the template
describing what it is

00:41:27.430 --> 00:41:30.910
going to do that shows which
billing account, which folder,

00:41:30.910 --> 00:41:33.520
and which policy and
labels are going to be set.

00:41:33.520 --> 00:41:35.620
It has created the project.

00:41:35.620 --> 00:41:39.070
It has set the IAM
policy, enabled billing,

00:41:39.070 --> 00:41:40.390
and created labels.

00:41:40.390 --> 00:41:42.040
And there's a link
right here that if I

00:41:42.040 --> 00:41:44.170
want to load up the
project on my browser just

00:41:44.170 --> 00:41:48.210
to make sure it did the
right thing, I can do that.

00:41:48.210 --> 00:41:50.890
Boom, the project created.

00:41:50.890 --> 00:41:52.830
One other example I will
show just so everyone

00:41:52.830 --> 00:41:56.850
can feel the pain
of the provisioning

00:41:56.850 --> 00:41:58.480
process for Firecloud.

00:41:58.480 --> 00:42:02.760
This is the template
for Firecloud.

00:42:02.760 --> 00:42:06.720
We had about 15 different
APIs we had to enable.

00:42:06.720 --> 00:42:10.230
We had nine different
IAM bindings

00:42:10.230 --> 00:42:11.700
that we had to add in there.

00:42:11.700 --> 00:42:15.360
We had to create three different
service accounts and so forth.

00:42:15.360 --> 00:42:17.820
So now all of this can be
done with a single command

00:42:17.820 --> 00:42:20.100
line where I specify
the names of all

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:22.980
50 projects and the
template that I want to use,

00:42:22.980 --> 00:42:26.250
and then I walk away and
it does it automatically.

00:42:26.250 --> 00:42:27.780
And that's the end of my demo.

