00:46:01.760 --> 00:46:05.660
It lives on GitHub, built by
the folks over at HashiCorp.

00:46:05.660 --> 00:46:07.176
That's where I work.

00:46:07.176 --> 00:46:08.050
And we're here today.

00:46:08.050 --> 00:46:10.550
If you want to come say hi or
you want to ask any questions,

00:46:10.550 --> 00:46:11.630
stop over by Charlie six.

00:46:11.630 --> 00:46:13.850
We'll be there all
day and tomorrow.

00:46:13.850 --> 00:46:15.680
TerraForm is designed,
like Rae said,

00:46:15.680 --> 00:46:17.810
for those
multi-cloud/hybrid-cloud

00:46:17.810 --> 00:46:18.780
situations.

00:46:18.780 --> 00:46:21.680
So it's very rare today to
have all of your resources

00:46:21.680 --> 00:46:23.426
in one cloud,
especially if you're

00:46:23.426 --> 00:46:25.550
a company that's been around
the block for a while.

00:46:25.550 --> 00:46:27.470
You probably have some
on-premise infrastructure.

00:46:27.470 --> 00:46:29.870
You probably have some stuff
in those other cloud providers.

00:46:29.870 --> 00:46:31.550
You probably have
some stuff in Google.

00:46:31.550 --> 00:46:33.091
And you really need
a way to describe

00:46:33.091 --> 00:46:35.240
all of those
infrastructure resources.

00:46:35.240 --> 00:46:37.460
What TerraForm
does is it provides

00:46:37.460 --> 00:46:39.290
a single configuration
file format,

00:46:39.290 --> 00:46:42.860
a text file, that allows you to
describe all of those resources

00:46:42.860 --> 00:46:47.240
across multiple clouds and
other things like top level DNS.

00:46:47.240 --> 00:46:48.860
Maybe you're using
a third party DNS

00:46:48.860 --> 00:46:52.160
provider or DDoS mitigation, and
you need to codify all of that

00:46:52.160 --> 00:46:53.450
into a file.

00:46:53.450 --> 00:46:57.890
And when we put that into a
file, what we get is theft.

00:46:57.890 --> 00:47:00.590
We get to steal everything that
application developers have

00:47:00.590 --> 00:47:04.010
had for years and apply
it to infrastructure.

00:47:04.010 --> 00:47:06.350
Because application engineers
have these great things.

00:47:06.350 --> 00:47:07.010
They have CI.

00:47:07.010 --> 00:47:07.640
They have CD.

00:47:07.640 --> 00:47:08.390
They have linting.

00:47:08.390 --> 00:47:10.280
They have testing--
that when you're just

00:47:10.280 --> 00:47:11.780
running shell
commands, you don't

00:47:11.780 --> 00:47:13.340
get that same level
of automation,

00:47:13.340 --> 00:47:15.080
that same level of testing.

00:47:15.080 --> 00:47:18.920
So TerraForm enables things like
testing, automation, project

00:47:18.920 --> 00:47:19.610
stamping.

00:47:19.610 --> 00:47:22.250
You get VCS and
GitHub integration.

00:47:22.250 --> 00:47:24.770
You can keep track of history
of all of your resources

00:47:24.770 --> 00:47:26.150
across the infrastructure.

00:47:26.150 --> 00:47:29.870
And because TerraForm has
a common state format,

00:47:29.870 --> 00:47:33.120
you can build APIs and
automation around that.

00:47:33.120 --> 00:47:34.790
So what are some use cases here?

00:47:34.790 --> 00:47:37.252
Well, many people run in
multiple environments.

00:47:37.252 --> 00:47:39.710
You have dev, you have prod,
you have QA, you have staging.

00:47:39.710 --> 00:47:42.920
And how many people here have
a long running QA environment

00:47:42.920 --> 00:47:45.534
that costs them thousands
of dollars a month?

00:47:45.534 --> 00:47:46.440
Yeah.

00:47:46.440 --> 00:47:49.380
And how many people here
never use that environment?

00:47:49.380 --> 00:47:51.870
It's just sitting there
wasting resources?

00:47:51.870 --> 00:47:53.520
Or you have the
opposite problem.

00:47:53.520 --> 00:47:55.587
Maybe you have this one
dedicated QA environment,

00:47:55.587 --> 00:47:58.170
but all of your developers are
queued up, waiting for changes,

00:47:58.170 --> 00:47:59.670
waiting to get
approval, and they're

00:47:59.670 --> 00:48:02.780
sitting in a large queue and
you're wasting resources.

00:48:02.780 --> 00:48:05.460
TerraForm can actually solve
both of these problems.

00:48:05.460 --> 00:48:07.770
Because TerraForm builds
a graph of your resources

00:48:07.770 --> 00:48:09.240
in these configuration
formats, it

00:48:09.240 --> 00:48:12.300
can quickly stamp out entire
environments, sometimes

00:48:12.300 --> 00:48:13.692
in less than five minutes.

00:48:13.692 --> 00:48:15.900
So instead of having this
long running QA environment

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:18.600
that's costing you thousands
of dollars a month,

00:48:18.600 --> 00:48:19.830
just spin one up.

00:48:19.830 --> 00:48:21.180
In the amount of time
it takes your developer

00:48:21.180 --> 00:48:22.680
to walk to the water
cooler and back, they

00:48:22.680 --> 00:48:24.930
could have a full development
environment ready to go.

00:48:24.930 --> 00:48:27.180
Send the URL off to the
QA team, the QA team

00:48:27.180 --> 00:48:31.500
stamps it, you destroy the
project, maybe it cost you $25.

00:48:31.500 --> 00:48:33.180
And this can all be
done in parallel.

00:48:33.180 --> 00:48:34.680
If you have multiple
QA engineers,

00:48:34.680 --> 00:48:36.840
you can have multiple
people approving deployments

00:48:36.840 --> 00:48:38.173
and they can go out in parallel.

00:48:38.173 --> 00:48:41.590
So it really ties into those
DevOps and Agile practices.

00:48:41.590 --> 00:48:43.590
The same thing can be
applied for things like CI

00:48:43.590 --> 00:48:44.790
and project stamping.

00:48:44.790 --> 00:48:46.860
Maybe you're an IT
team and you need

00:48:46.860 --> 00:48:50.040
to give different organizations
exactly the same thing

00:48:50.040 --> 00:48:51.390
over and over and over again.

00:48:51.390 --> 00:48:52.750
But it's multi-cloud.

00:48:52.750 --> 00:48:53.860
How would you do that?

00:48:53.860 --> 00:48:56.136
Well, with TerraForm you
define the configurations,

00:48:56.136 --> 00:48:57.510
you change one or
two attributes,

00:48:57.510 --> 00:48:59.550
and you can very
quickly stamp out

00:48:59.550 --> 00:49:02.770
different projects for different
teams in the organization.

00:49:02.770 --> 00:49:04.890
And lastly, continuous
integration.

00:49:04.890 --> 00:49:05.970
Everyone loves Jenkins.

00:49:05.970 --> 00:49:07.980
It's one of the most
popular CI tools.

00:49:07.980 --> 00:49:11.100
But we tend to have these really
long running CI environments,

00:49:11.100 --> 00:49:13.170
and they gradually become
snowflakes over time.

00:49:13.170 --> 00:49:15.360
Or we have different tools
installed and different

00:49:15.360 --> 00:49:17.975
plug-ins and widgets,
and we run them overnight

00:49:17.975 --> 00:49:20.100
even though we don't have
any CI running overnight.

00:49:20.100 --> 00:49:22.290
So we're paying for
them to live on forever.

00:49:22.290 --> 00:49:23.910
With TerraForm you
can codify those.

00:49:23.910 --> 00:49:25.830
You can quickly scale them
up, quickly scale them down,

00:49:25.830 --> 00:49:27.150
and turn them off overnight.

00:49:27.150 --> 00:49:29.980
So you're not paying for
those unused resources.

00:49:29.980 --> 00:49:31.950
So I want to quickly
go through an example

00:49:31.950 --> 00:49:33.702
here for project templating.

00:49:33.702 --> 00:49:35.160
The best way to
think about this is

00:49:35.160 --> 00:49:36.660
some of the previous
examples we saw

00:49:36.660 --> 00:49:39.570
where you have training
environments or isolated

00:49:39.570 --> 00:49:41.030
projects that you
want to create.

00:49:41.030 --> 00:49:42.530
This is the workflow
you saw before.

00:49:42.530 --> 00:49:44.696
If you were doing this in
the UI or through the API,

00:49:44.696 --> 00:49:46.020
you'd have to create a project.

00:49:46.020 --> 00:49:47.853
You'd have to enable
billing on that object,

00:49:47.853 --> 00:49:50.620
then enable the APIs, optionally
create service accounts,

00:49:50.620 --> 00:49:52.980
set IAM policies-- that same
workflow that Lukas talked

00:49:52.980 --> 00:49:54.563
about that involves
clicking buttons--

00:49:54.563 --> 00:49:57.510
and it's relatively
painful to do manually.

00:49:57.510 --> 00:49:59.195
Thankfully, Google
has a number of APIs

00:49:59.195 --> 00:50:01.650
and TerraForm
consumes those APIs.

00:50:01.650 --> 00:50:03.805
So this is an example
TerraForm configuration.

00:50:03.805 --> 00:50:05.430
And just like whenever
you need to talk

00:50:05.430 --> 00:50:08.599
to Google's API with cURL
or with the command line,

00:50:08.599 --> 00:50:10.140
you have to give it
your credentials.

00:50:10.140 --> 00:50:12.150
It can consume the local
G Cloud credentials

00:50:12.150 --> 00:50:14.700
or you can provide them
via an account file.

00:50:14.700 --> 00:50:16.200
So here we're just
telling TerraForm

00:50:16.200 --> 00:50:18.420
how to talk to Google.

00:50:18.420 --> 00:50:21.072
Next, we have to define
what's called a project.

00:50:21.072 --> 00:50:22.530
This can be created
multiple times,

00:50:22.530 --> 00:50:24.070
so this is creating
a project that's

00:50:24.070 --> 00:50:26.700
named terraform-demo-123456.

00:50:26.700 --> 00:50:28.860
I'm attaching it to
a billing account,

00:50:28.860 --> 00:50:30.900
and then I'm putting it
inside my organization.

00:50:30.900 --> 00:50:34.350
And I'm just giving it the
human-friendly label of demo.

00:50:34.350 --> 00:50:35.790
I could create
multiple projects.

00:50:35.790 --> 00:50:38.190
I could put this piece
of code multiple times

00:50:38.190 --> 00:50:40.620
on the same slide to
create multiple projects.

00:50:40.620 --> 00:50:42.540
Or I could use a count
attribute to create

00:50:42.540 --> 00:50:46.500
10 projects all at once.

00:50:46.500 --> 00:50:48.917
Within that project I then
have to enable services.

00:50:48.917 --> 00:50:51.000
By default, if you haven't
configured the org node

00:50:51.000 --> 00:50:54.270
when you create a project, it
doesn't have any APIs enabled.

00:50:54.270 --> 00:50:56.160
So we can use the Google
project resource--

00:50:56.160 --> 00:50:59.040
Google project services
resource, to enable services

00:50:59.040 --> 00:51:00.120
on that project.

00:51:00.120 --> 00:51:02.970
The project is the name of
the project I just created,

00:51:02.970 --> 00:51:05.865
and then here I'm enabling IAM
and the Cloud Resource Manager

00:51:05.865 --> 00:51:06.365
APIs.

00:51:10.300 --> 00:51:12.490
Then we can optionally
create a service account.

00:51:12.490 --> 00:51:14.140
So that service
account here is going

00:51:14.140 --> 00:51:15.760
to be created
inside that project.

00:51:15.760 --> 00:51:18.370
I'm giving it a human-friendly
name of demo service account,

00:51:18.370 --> 00:51:20.440
and I'm attaching
it to my account.

00:51:20.440 --> 00:51:22.820
This service account has
no permission, though.

00:51:22.820 --> 00:51:25.870
So what we have to do is we
have to generate an IAM policy.

00:51:25.870 --> 00:51:28.090
Here I'm using
TerraForm's data source

00:51:28.090 --> 00:51:30.820
to generate an IAM policy
that grants the editor

00:51:30.820 --> 00:51:34.060
role to my service account, the
service account I just created.

00:51:34.060 --> 00:51:36.490
And it's granting me owner
role over the project,

00:51:36.490 --> 00:51:38.050
so I'll be able to be an owner.

00:51:38.050 --> 00:51:40.990
And you can specify this binding
and these roles multiple times

00:51:40.990 --> 00:51:42.384
to create different memberships.

00:51:42.384 --> 00:51:44.050
You can get as fine
grained as you like,

00:51:44.050 --> 00:51:46.600
and we saw a lot of examples
earlier of fine grained IAM

00:51:46.600 --> 00:51:48.430
permissions.

00:51:48.430 --> 00:51:51.040
And then lastly, we have
to upload that policy

00:51:51.040 --> 00:51:52.660
to Google Cloud.

00:51:52.660 --> 00:51:55.780
So we create an IAM policy,
we assign it to our project,

00:51:55.780 --> 00:51:58.630
and we give it that policy data.

00:51:58.630 --> 00:52:01.720
So now we've templatized
and programmatically created

00:52:01.720 --> 00:52:03.400
a project with a
service account,

00:52:03.400 --> 00:52:07.960
with the APIs we needed, all in
a single configuration format.

00:52:07.960 --> 00:52:10.364
And this could be
across multiple clouds.

00:52:10.364 --> 00:52:12.280
What you're seeing here
with this dollar curly

00:52:12.280 --> 00:52:15.760
brace syntax is TerraForm's
interpolation syntax.

00:52:15.760 --> 00:52:17.800
TerraForm builds a
graph under the hood

00:52:17.800 --> 00:52:19.820
by using this
interpolation syntax.

00:52:19.820 --> 00:52:21.640
So it'll parallelize
these operations

00:52:21.640 --> 00:52:24.160
and create these resources
as fast as possible.

00:52:24.160 --> 00:52:25.780
So if you're
creating 10 projects

00:52:25.780 --> 00:52:27.820
and your quota allows
for you to have

00:52:27.820 --> 00:52:29.740
10 projects created
simultaneously,

00:52:29.740 --> 00:52:31.330
TerraForm will do that for you.

00:52:31.330 --> 00:52:33.220
If you have a big huge
quota, because you're

00:52:33.220 --> 00:52:34.990
someone like the Broad
Institute where you're

00:52:34.990 --> 00:52:36.190
creating lots and
lots of projects,

00:52:36.190 --> 00:52:38.020
you can create all
of those in parallel.

00:52:38.020 --> 00:52:40.810
So provisioning times can be
as low as five to 10 minutes

00:52:40.810 --> 00:52:42.620
for some of these resources.

00:52:42.620 --> 00:52:44.639
You can also manage
these changes over time.

00:52:44.639 --> 00:52:46.180
So if this project
changes, maybe you

00:52:46.180 --> 00:52:47.140
want to update the name of it.

00:52:47.140 --> 00:52:49.604
You want to change the service
account or the IAM policy.

00:52:49.604 --> 00:52:50.770
You change it in the config.

00:52:50.770 --> 00:52:52.270
You check it into
source control.

00:52:52.270 --> 00:52:55.249
You get the pull request
approval workflow.

00:52:55.249 --> 00:52:57.040
Then you apply those
changes to production,

00:52:57.040 --> 00:52:59.960
and TerraForm handles
the diff for you.

00:52:59.960 --> 00:53:01.870
And then lastly, this
isn't new to TerraForm,

00:53:01.870 --> 00:53:03.870
this is something that
has existed for a while--

00:53:03.870 --> 00:53:05.380
the ability to manage resources.

00:53:05.380 --> 00:53:07.300
But within those
projects, you can now

00:53:07.300 --> 00:53:09.610
specify that you want to
resource to live inside

00:53:09.610 --> 00:53:11.030
of a particular project.

00:53:11.030 --> 00:53:12.970
So here I'm creating
a compute instance,

00:53:12.970 --> 00:53:15.460
and I'm saying this compute
instance lives in the project

00:53:15.460 --> 00:53:16.820
that I just created.

00:53:16.820 --> 00:53:18.950
So that TerraForm demo
project I just created,

00:53:18.950 --> 00:53:20.740
we're dropping this
resource in there.

00:53:20.740 --> 00:53:22.720
And if we had used
account attribute,

00:53:22.720 --> 00:53:24.460
we would be able to
create this resource

00:53:24.460 --> 00:53:26.350
in every single
project that we create,

00:53:26.350 --> 00:53:28.266
allowing us to build
something like a training

00:53:28.266 --> 00:53:30.100
environment or
ephemeral environments

00:53:30.100 --> 00:53:33.830
for staging QA, dev,
whatever it might be.

00:53:33.830 --> 00:53:36.040
So the goal here,
like the vision,

00:53:36.040 --> 00:53:39.370
is to enable reproducible,
isolated, and codified

00:53:39.370 --> 00:53:42.290
environments, regardless
of your cloud provider,

00:53:42.290 --> 00:53:45.100
your multi-cloud, or your
hybrid cloud situation.

00:53:45.100 --> 00:53:48.550
When we enable the ability to
capture infrastructure as code,

00:53:48.550 --> 00:53:51.070
we get all of the benefits
that application developers

00:53:51.070 --> 00:53:55.090
have had for years, while
we get the reproducibility,

00:53:55.090 --> 00:53:57.520
the multi-cloud and the
hybrid-cloud approaches,

00:53:57.520 --> 00:54:00.250
that allow us to be highly
scalable and highly agile

00:54:00.250 --> 00:54:02.110
in today's multi-cloud
environments.

00:54:02.110 --> 00:54:03.436
Thank you.

00:54:03.436 --> 00:54:09.112
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:09.112 --> 00:54:10.340
RAE WANG: Thank you, Seth.

00:54:10.340 --> 00:54:12.490
And this brings us to
the end of our talk.

00:54:12.490 --> 00:54:14.140
Just to summarize,
over the last hour

00:54:14.140 --> 00:54:16.990
we went through how to use
IAM roles and permissions

00:54:16.990 --> 00:54:19.570
to achieve the principle
of least privilege.

00:54:19.570 --> 00:54:22.660
Using resource hierarchy to
both have central control

00:54:22.660 --> 00:54:25.810
and delegate autonomy
to independent teams.

00:54:25.810 --> 00:54:28.480
Org policies can allow
admin to set guardrails

00:54:28.480 --> 00:54:31.060
around the flexibilities,
and automation

00:54:31.060 --> 00:54:33.790
is key for scalability
and repeatability.

00:54:33.790 --> 00:54:35.950
And finally, using
open source tools,

00:54:35.950 --> 00:54:38.020
you can leverage of
these capabilities

00:54:38.020 --> 00:54:40.850
but customized for
your own environments.

00:54:40.850 --> 00:54:43.195
So this is the slide
for your photo needs.

00:54:43.195 --> 00:54:45.640
It's a summary of all the
launches we have mentioned

00:54:45.640 --> 00:54:47.960
and where to learn more.

00:54:47.960 --> 00:54:50.990
We have a few minutes left,
so we could do some questions.

00:54:50.990 --> 00:54:53.080
There are two microphones
in the middle.

00:54:53.080 --> 00:54:55.970
We're also going to be at the
meet a PM send box afterwards,

00:54:55.970 --> 00:54:58.584
so if you want to come and chat
in detail that can work, too.

00:55:11.080 --> 00:55:11.580
OK.

00:55:11.580 --> 00:55:12.780
Thank you.

00:55:12.780 --> 00:55:13.980
[APPLAUSE]

00:55:14.580 --> 00:55:17.330
